# Compact code

Prefer arrow / expression-bodied members and compact code over verbose blocks.

- Use expression-bodied members (`=>`) for single-expression methods, properties, and constructors when the body is a lookup, assignment, throw, or simple return.
- Collapse `if`/`return` or `if`/`throw` into a ternary or `throw` expression when it stays readable.
- Do not expand a one-liner into a braced block unless there are multiple statements, meaningful locals, or the expression becomes harder to read.
- Keep types and APIs small; avoid extra wrapper types when a parameter or existing type is enough.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
public static IDatasource Create(string provider, object? options = null)
{
    if (!Factories.TryGetValue(provider, out var factory))
    {
        throw new ProviderNotInstalledException(provider);
    }

    return factory.Create(options);
}

// ✅ GOOD
public static IDatasource Create(string provider, object? options = null) =>
    Factories.TryGetValue(provider, out var factory)
        ? factory.Create(options)
        : throw new ProviderNotInstalledException(provider);
```
